# Speedat - web-root .htaccess (Hostinger public_html).  DEPLOYMENT REFERENCE COPY.
#
# WHICH FILE IS LIVE
#   bin/build-release.php ships php/public/.htaccess as public_html/.htaccess.
#   That file is canonical. THIS file is the same rules, line for line, plus
#   the blocks that are deliberately switched OFF by default - the
#   http->https redirect and the ModSecurity note - each with the reason it is
#   off and the test that says whether to turn it on. Paste from here through
#   hPanel -> File Manager when one of those has to be turned on on the host.
#   It is also the builder's fallback if php/public/.htaccess ever goes
#   missing, which is one more reason it must not say anything different.
#
# HOW THE TWO ARE KEPT IN STEP
#   They drifted badly once: this copy had kept the old case-sensitive deny
#   patterns, a 2.2 block that missed half the names, no Authorization
#   pass-through, and active rules (a method block, an `immutable` cache
#   header on every asset, Cache-Control on index.php) that the live server
#   never ran - so pasting from here would have changed the site in ways
#   nobody had tested. Reconciled 2026-10-26 by making this copy
#   php/public/.htaccess plus commented blocks. tests/Core/HtaccessTest fails
#   if the ACTIVE (non-comment) lines of the two files differ in any way, so
#   a change belongs in php/public/.htaccess first and is copied here after.
#   Everything below that is not a `#` line must stay identical; the rule and
#   reasoning comments are copied too, so either file reads on its own.
#
# THE OPTIONAL BLOCKS IN THIS COPY, all commented out
#   * force https             inside the mod_rewrite block, below
#   * HSTS                    in the security-headers block (also in the
#                             shipped file, also commented out)
#   * ModSecurity relief      the last section of this file

Options -Indexes -MultiViews
DirectoryIndex index.php

# --- the web server's own body cap ------------------------------------------
# 2 MiB, refused a layer EARLIER than everything else on this site.
#
# post_max_size (in .user.ini beside this file) stops a giant body before the
# application sees it, but PHP still has to be started, handed the request and
# asked to read it. LimitRequestBody is the web server refusing the body with a
# 413 before a PHP worker is engaged at all, which on a one-core shared plan is
# the difference between a refusal costing nothing and a refusal costing a
# worker for as long as the sender cares to keep sending.
#
# THE SIZE IS FOR A SITE WITH NO UPLOADS, WHICH IS WHAT THIS SITE IS. Not one
# form carries enctype="multipart/form-data" - the Excel rate sheet was removed
# (owner, 2026-09-27) - so the largest honest request is the rates editor's few
# hundred text fields, tens of kilobytes. 2 MiB is roughly fifty times that and
# sits UNDER post_max_size (3M) on purpose: whichever layer a host honours, the
# web server's cheaper refusal fires first and the PHP-side cap stays the
# backstop rather than the front line.
#
# WHY IT IS WRAPPED, WHEN LimitRequestBody IS CORE APACHE AND NOT A mod_*.
# Because rule 1 above is absolute: an unsupported directive here is a hard 500
# that takes the whole site down, and "core Apache" is a statement about
# Apache, not about LiteSpeed's .htaccess subset. `core.c` is the module
# identifier Apache itself answers to. A host that does not know that name gets
# the second block, which asks for the same cap on any server already proving
# it parses <IfModule> by giving us mod_rewrite - without which this site has
# no front controller and serves nothing at all.
#
# A host matching NEITHER sets no cap here and does not 500, and post_max_size
# in .user.ini still refuses the body. That is the correct direction to fail:
# a missing optimisation, never a missing site.
<IfModule core.c>
    LimitRequestBody 2097152
</IfModule>
<IfModule !core.c>
    <IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
        LimitRequestBody 2097152
    </IfModule>
</IfModule>

# --- put the Authorization header back where PHP can see it ------------------
# MEASURED, not assumed. On the staging host (Apache 2.4.68, mod_php 8.2.34) a
# request carrying `Authorization: Bearer <token>` reached PHP with the header
# present in apache_request_headers() and with NOTHING in $_SERVER - no
# HTTP_AUTHORIZATION, no anything. mod_php builds $_SERVER from Apache's
# subprocess_env, and ap_add_common_vars() deliberately leaves Authorization and
# Proxy-Authorization out of it unless CGIPassAuth is on, so that a CGI script
# cannot read credentials for a request it is not itself authenticating. The
# effect here was that /api/metrics answered 401 to a CORRECT bearer token while
# accepting the same secret in a query string - so the only shape that worked
# was the one that writes the secret into every access log between the caller
# and this site.
#
# SetEnvIf RATHER THAN CGIPassAuth. CGIPassAuth is the directive meant for this
# and is the right one on a host that has it - but it only exists from Apache
# 2.4.13, it is CORE rather than a mod_*, and rule 1 at the top of this file is
# absolute: a directive the server does not know is a hard 500, and a 500 here
# is the whole site. <IfModule> can ask "do you have this module", never "do you
# know this directive", and core.c answers yes on 2.4.10 too. mod_setenvif is
# ancient, universally present, and wrapped anyway.
#
# THIS IS THE CHEAP LAYER AND NOT THE ONLY ONE.
# Core\Request::authorizationHeader() reads the value back from
# REDIRECT_HTTP_AUTHORIZATION - which is where the rewrite below re-exposes what
# this line sets - AND from apache_request_headers() directly, so a host that
# ignores this file entirely still authenticates.
<IfModule mod_setenvif.c>
    SetEnvIf Authorization "(.*)" HTTP_AUTHORIZATION=$1
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On

    # Hostinger serves the site from the account's document root.
    RewriteBase /

    # Never rewrite something that really exists.
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f [OR]
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
    RewriteRule ^ - [L]

    # --- OPTIONAL: force https ---------------------------------------------
    # LEAVE THIS OFF on Hostinger. Free certificates are installed
    # automatically and the platform already forces https; adding a second
    # redirect here gives every visitor two round trips, and gets it wrong
    # behind a proxy that terminates TLS (%{HTTPS} reads "off" on the origin
    # even though the browser is on https, which is an infinite redirect).
    #
    # Turn it on ONLY if you have verified with curl -I that a plain http://
    # request is answered 200 instead of 301. Then uncomment all three lines,
    # here AND in php/public/.htaccess - an active line in only one of the two
    # fails tests/Core/HtaccessTest.
    #
    # RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
    # RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
    # RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

    # Everything else is the application's problem.
    RewriteRule ^ index.php [L]
</IfModule>

# --- deny everything that is not meant to be downloaded ---------------------
# The two blocks below deny exactly the same five things. They are kept
# character-for-character identical in their patterns so that a change to one
# is obviously a change to the other:
#
#   A. ^\.                        dotfiles: .env, .git*, .htpasswd, .user.ini
#   B. \.(sql|md|log|ini|...)$    source, dumps, configuration, backups, keys
#   C. (~|\.(save|rej|...))$      editor droppings with no known extension
#   D. ^(config|secrets|...)\.json  credentials that arrive as .json
#   E. router.php                 the dev server's rewrite, never used here
#
# EVERY PATTERN STARTS WITH (?i), AND THAT IS NOT DECORATION. <FilesMatch> is
# case-sensitive by default, the host filesystem is Linux, and Linux is
# case-sensitive too - so `Config.PHP`, `DUMP.SQL` and `Secrets.JSON` are three
# different filenames that the case-sensitive versions of these patterns served
# as plain text. A release builder with the same bug shipped a file containing
# the whole production configuration under the name `Settings.LOCAL.php`
# (audit round 1, finding 7), which is what the name rules here are the second
# lock for.
#
# Apache 2.4 / LiteSpeed: mod_authz_core.
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    <FilesMatch "(?i)^\.">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
    <FilesMatch "(?i)\.(sql|sqlite|sqlite3|db|md|log|ini|yml|yaml|lock|sh|bak|dist|sample|twig|tpl|pem|key|p12|pfx)$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
    # Editor and merge droppings whose name ends in something that is not an
    # extension at all: index.php~, config.php.save, routes.php.rej,
    # index.php.orig~. None of these matched the extension list above and none
    # of them starts with a dot, so LiteSpeed served them as plain text - the
    # source of a file whose live version PHP would have executed.
    <FilesMatch "(?i)(~|\.(save|rej|tmp|temp|old|copy|orig|swp|swo))$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
    # config.json / credentials.json style files, but not the site manifests.
    <FilesMatch "(?i)^(config|secrets|credentials|firebase|service-account).*\.json$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
    # <Files> matches a literal name, so it used to miss Router.PHP. FilesMatch
    # with (?i) is the same rule spelled in a way the host's case-sensitivity
    # cannot walk around.
    <FilesMatch "(?i)^router\.php$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Apache 2.2 syntax, for a host whose authorisation is mod_access_compat. The
# inner <IfModule> matters as much as the outer one: on a host with neither
# module a bare `Order` line is a 500, and a 500 here is the whole site.
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    <IfModule mod_access_compat.c>
        <FilesMatch "(?i)^\.">
            Order allow,deny
            Deny from all
        </FilesMatch>
        <FilesMatch "(?i)\.(sql|sqlite|sqlite3|db|md|log|ini|yml|yaml|lock|sh|bak|dist|sample|twig|tpl|pem|key|p12|pfx)$">
            Order allow,deny
            Deny from all
        </FilesMatch>
        <FilesMatch "(?i)(~|\.(save|rej|tmp|temp|old|copy|orig|swp|swo))$">
            Order allow,deny
            Deny from all
        </FilesMatch>
        <FilesMatch "(?i)^(config|secrets|credentials|firebase|service-account).*\.json$">
            Order allow,deny
            Deny from all
        </FilesMatch>
        <FilesMatch "(?i)^router\.php$">
            Order allow,deny
            Deny from all
        </FilesMatch>
    </IfModule>
</IfModule>

# --- security headers -------------------------------------------------------
# ONE COPY OF EACH HEADER, FROM THIS FILE, ON BOTH SERVERS.
#
# app/Core/App.php sets the same five headers from PHP, because a host that
# ignores this file must still send them. When this file IS honoured, the value
# below is the one that must reach the browser, and it must reach it ONCE:
#
#   LiteSpeed (Hostinger, the live host). `Header always set` replaces PHP's
#     value - measured 2026-09-30, one Permissions-Policy line and it was this
#     file's string. Nothing to add, and nothing below changes it: the
#     <IfModule !LiteSpeed> block is skipped there, because LiteSpeed answers
#     to the module name `LiteSpeed` (the same test LSCache writes into
#     WordPress .htaccess files).
#   Apache + mod_php (the staging host, the go-live rehearsal, the sim).
#     Apache keeps two header tables. `Header always set` writes
#     err_headers_out; PHP's header() writes headers_out; BOTH go on the wire.
#     Measured on the sim (Apache 2.4.69, PHP 8.2.35, 2026-10-26): all five
#     headers twice. Mostly harmless duplicates - except Cross-Origin-Opener-
#     Policy, which a browser parses as ONE token: two of them read as
#     "same-origin-allow-popups, same-origin-allow-popups", fail to parse, and
#     fall back to unsafe-none - no COOP at all. A plain `Header unset` (no
#     `always`) acts on headers_out only, so it removes PHP's copy and leaves
#     this file's. The two tables are separate, so their order does not matter
#     on Apache; the unset is still written FIRST in case some other server
#     ever runs them as one list in file order, where unset-then-set still
#     ends with exactly one value.
#
# Why not the other way round - drop these lines and let PHP send them? Because
# this copy is the only one a request that never reaches PHP carries: a static
# file, a 403 from the deny rules below, a 413 from LimitRequestBody.
#
# The values are identical to App.php's, so whichever layer answers says the
# same thing; tests/Security/HeaderParityTest fails if they drift, if a name is
# set twice here, or if one loses its Apache-only unset.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    <IfModule !LiteSpeed>
        Header unset X-Content-Type-Options
        Header unset X-Frame-Options
        Header unset Referrer-Policy
        Header unset Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
        Header unset Permissions-Policy
    </IfModule>
    Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    # DENY, matching app/Core/App.php and the CSP's `frame-ancestors 'none'`.
    # This copy is the one that answers on a request that dies before PHP sets
    # its own headers, so a weaker value here is not a duplicate - it is the
    # only header that static file, that 500 and that directory index carry.
    Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
    Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
    # COOP and Permissions-Policy are spelled EXACTLY as app/Core/App.php
    # spells them. On Hostinger this `Header always set` runs after PHP and
    # wins (measured 2026-09-30), so this copy is the live value, not a
    # fallback. same-origin-allow-popups because plain same-origin breaks
    # Firebase's Google sign-in popup (popup-closed-by-user on every try).
    # tests/Security/HeaderParityTest fails if the three copies drift.
    Header always set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin-allow-popups"
    Header always set Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=(), browsing-topics=(), interest-cohort=()"

    # HSTS: only meaningful once the domain is HTTPS-only. Hostinger issues a
    # free certificate and forces https, so this is safe there. It is commented
    # out until the PHP site is actually the live site, because a wrong HSTS
    # header is remembered by browsers and is painful to undo.
    # Turning it on here means turning on BOTH lines, for the same two-table
    # reason as above: App.php also sends HSTS when security.hsts is true, and
    # without the unset an Apache host would send it twice.
    # Header unset Strict-Transport-Security
    # Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

    # PHP's version banner, X-Powered-By: PHP/8.2.x. BOTH lines, one per table.
    # PHP writes it into headers_out like any other header() call, so on Apache
    # `Header always unset` (err_headers_out) never saw it and the sim answered
    # X-Powered-By: PHP/8.2.35 on every page; the plain `Header unset` is the
    # line that removes it there. `always unset` stays for the error responses
    # Apache builds itself. Unconditional, unlike the block above: removing a
    # header that is not there is a no-op on every server.
    #
    # NOT expose_php. That setting is PHP_INI_SYSTEM: php_flag in .htaccess and
    # .user.ini both ignore it without a word, and only the host's php.ini
    # (hPanel -> PHP Configuration) can turn it off. The `php_flag expose_php
    # Off` lines that used to sit in the mod_php blocks below did nothing and
    # read as protection, so they are gone. App::send() also calls
    # header_remove('X-Powered-By'), for a host that ignores this file.
    Header unset X-Powered-By
    Header always unset X-Powered-By
</IfModule>

# --- MIME + compression -----------------------------------------------------
# woff2's registered media type is `font/woff2` (RFC 8081). This line used to
# declare `application/font-woff2` - a pre-RFC name - while the expiry block
# below gave its year to `font/woff2`, so the two never referred to the same
# thing and every self-hosted font fell through to ExpiresDefault "access plus 0
# seconds": refetched on every page, on the mobile target this site is built for,
# with nothing visibly wrong (audit round 1, finding 18). The registered name is
# declared here and BOTH names are given the year below, because a host may
# already know the legacy one and win.
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    AddDefaultCharset UTF-8
    AddType font/woff2 .woff2
    AddType font/woff .woff
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/plain text/css text/javascript application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml
</IfModule>

# --- static asset caching ---------------------------------------------------
# HTML is never cached: prices change and a stale quote page is a wrong quote.
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
    ExpiresActive On
    ExpiresDefault "access plus 0 seconds"
    ExpiresByType text/html "access plus 0 seconds"
    ExpiresByType text/css "access plus 7 days"
    ExpiresByType application/javascript "access plus 7 days"
    ExpiresByType image/svg+xml "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/webp "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/png "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/jpeg "access plus 30 days"
    # The favicon, under both of its names: Apache's mime.types says
    # image/vnd.microsoft.icon, older tables and LiteSpeed say image/x-icon.
    # Neither was listed, so /favicon.ico fell to ExpiresDefault and came back
    # `Cache-Control: max-age=0` (measured on the sim, 2026-10-26) - fetched
    # again on every page view by every browser that asks for it, which is all
    # of them. The deploy copy had the x-icon line and this file did not.
    ExpiresByType image/x-icon "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/vnd.microsoft.icon "access plus 30 days"
    # text/javascript is the type RFC 9239 made the registered one; a host whose
    # table already uses it would otherwise give every script max-age=0.
    ExpiresByType text/javascript "access plus 7 days"
    # Static .txt files - llms.txt, and the font and flag LICENSE.txt - one day.
    # BY FILE NAME, NOT `ExpiresByType text/plain`, and that is deliberate.
    # mod_expires acts on PHP's output too (it merged max-age=0 into the HTML
    # pages' own Cache-Control on the sim), and index.php answers text/plain
    # for robots.txt, /health, /api/metrics and plain-text errors. A type rule
    # would hand a day's cache to a 500. A rewritten request's file is
    # index.php, so this pattern only ever meets a real .txt on disk. One day,
    # not thirty: llms.txt is edited by hand and should not stay stale long.
    <FilesMatch "(?i)\.txt$">
        ExpiresDefault "access plus 1 day"
    </FilesMatch>
    # Both spellings of the same file type - see the mod_mime block above.
    ExpiresByType font/woff2 "access plus 365 days"
    ExpiresByType application/font-woff2 "access plus 365 days"
    ExpiresByType font/woff "access plus 365 days"
    ExpiresByType application/font-woff "access plus 365 days"
</IfModule>

# --- PHP settings the host may allow us to set ------------------------------
# On Hostinger these are normally set in hPanel's PHP configuration or in
# .user.ini (see deploy/htaccess/user.ini.sample) instead. php_flag/php_value
# only work under mod_php; LiteSpeed/PHP-FPM ignores them silently, which is
# why they are wrapped and why nothing depends on them.
#
# 2M / 3M, AND THE SMALLNESS IS THE POINT.
# These were 8M/9M, sized for the Excel rate sheet. That feature was removed
# (owner, 2026-09-27) and NOTHING on this site uploads a file any more, so the
# largest legitimate body is the rates editor's few hundred text fields - tens
# of kilobytes. A 12 MB POST at the public contact form is not a big enquiry,
# it is somebody making a 1-core shared plan read and buffer twelve megabytes
# before a single line of application code decides anything. This is the layer
# that refuses it: PHP discards an over-sized body before the app is reached,
# which costs nothing, whereas every cap inside the application is spent after
# the bytes have already arrived.
#
# POST_MAX_SIZE STAYS LARGER THAN UPLOAD_MAX_FILESIZE, for the reason it always
# was: post_max_size caps the WHOLE body - a file, plus its multipart
# boundaries and headers, plus every other field, plus the CSRF token. With the
# two equal, a file at exactly the documented limit makes a body slightly over
# it and PHP throws the lot away: empty $_POST, empty $_FILES, no upload error
# to report, and a form that appears to have done nothing. 1M of headroom is
# hundreds of times what this site posts. Core\Upload::problem() says so in
# plain words if it ever happens anyway.
#
# max_input_vars 4000, the same number .user.ini sets. It is HERE as well as
# there because a host reads one file or the other and never says which: if the
# two disagree, the site's real limit is an accident of this host's SAPI rather
# than a number anybody chose. The largest form on the site is one country row
# in the rates editor - 98 named controls, measured 2026-09-29 against the real
# document - so 4000 is roughly forty times the worst honest case, which is the
# margin asked for because a body PHP cut short is not an error: $_POST simply
# ends early. (Http\Admin\DraftForm catches that and refuses the save, so this
# is headroom, not a dependency.) The reasoning in full is in the .user.ini.
#
# php_flag/php_value only work under mod_php; LiteSpeed/PHP-FPM ignores them
# silently. The file that actually applies on Hostinger is .user.ini - see
# deploy/htaccess/user.ini.sample, which carries the same values.
<IfModule mod_php.c>
    php_flag display_errors Off
    php_value upload_max_filesize 2M
    php_value post_max_size 3M
    php_value max_input_vars 4000
</IfModule>
<IfModule mod_php7.c>
    php_flag display_errors Off
    php_value upload_max_filesize 2M
    php_value post_max_size 3M
    php_value max_input_vars 4000
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL: ModSecurity relief for the rates admin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ModSecurity is ON by default on Hostinger and false-positives on exactly what
# a rates admin posts: long text, numbers in SQL-ish shapes, pasted HTML. The
# symptom is a 403 that never reaches PHP, so it is absent from storage/logs.
#
# Do NOT uncomment this pre-emptively. If and only if a specific admin POST
# 403s while the same request succeeds with a shorter body, narrow the rule to
# that ONE path - never the whole site.
#
# <IfModule mod_security.c>
#     <Location "/admin/rates">
#         SecRuleRemoveById 200003
#     </Location>
# </IfModule>
#
# (<Location> is invalid in .htaccess. On Hostinger the supported route is
#  hPanel -> Advanced -> ModSecurity, which toggles it per directory. Put the
#  admin under its own directory only if it comes to that.)
